---
import ArticleLayout from '../../components/ArticleLayout.astro';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'What is the average cost to turn over a rental in Nashville?',
    a: 'Most single-family and small multi-family turnovers in the Nashville area run $1,200 to $4,500, with a typical mid-range turn landing around $2,500 to $3,500. The two swing factors are flooring and paint. A light refresh can come in under $1,500; a full turn with new flooring and a repaint can pass $5,000.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What does a rental turnover actually include?',
    a: 'A standard turnover includes a move-out deep clean, paint (touch-up or full repaint), minor repairs from the punch list, carpet cleaning or replacement, and a final rent-ready inspection. Fuller turns add new flooring, cabinet painting, and fixture updates.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How long does a rental turnover take?',
    a: 'A coordinated turnover on a single-family rental typically takes 4 to 7 days when one crew handles every trade. It stretches to 3 weeks or more when a landlord books a cleaner, a painter, and a handyman separately and waits for each to schedule.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the biggest hidden cost of a turnover?',
    a: 'Lost rent. Every vacant day is rent you will never collect. On a $1,800/month rental that is about $60 per day, so shaving even a week off the turn is worth roughly $420, which often covers the difference between piecemeal contractors and one coordinated crew.',
  },
];
---
<ArticleLayout
  slug="rental-turnover-cost-nashville"
  eyebrow="Cost Guide"
  title="How Much Does a Rental Turnover Cost in Nashville? (2026 Breakdown)"
  description="A real line-item breakdown of what it costs to turn over a rental in Nashville and Middle Tennessee: cleaning, paint, flooring, repairs, and the vacancy cost most owners forget."
  faqs={faqs}
>
  <p>
    Most rental turnovers in the Nashville and Middle Tennessee area run
    <strong>$1,200 to $4,500</strong>, with a typical mid-range turn landing
    around <strong>$2,500 to $3,500</strong>. National data pegs the average
    apartment turnover near $3,900, but that number hides everything a
    landlord actually wants to know: what drives it, where it can be trimmed,
    and the one cost most owners forget to count. Here is the real breakdown.
  </p>

  <h2>The line-item breakdown</h2>
  <p>
    Every turnover is a stack of smaller jobs. Here is what each piece
    typically costs on a single-family or small multi-family rental in the
    Nashville area, for a standard turn between tenants.
  </p>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Line item</th><th>Typical range</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Move-out deep clean</td><td>$200 – $500</td><td>Scales with unit size and condition</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Paint (touch-up)</td><td>$300 – $600</td><td>Scuffs, marks, high-traffic walls</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Paint (full interior repaint)</td><td>$1,000 – $2,500</td><td>The single biggest swing item</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Carpet cleaning</td><td>$120 – $300</td><td>If carpet is salvageable</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Carpet replacement</td><td>$1.50 – $4.00 / sq ft</td><td>When cleaning will not save it</td></tr>
        <tr><td>LVP flooring (installed)</td><td>$4 – $9 / sq ft</td><td>The rental workhorse; outlasts carpet</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Punch-list repairs</td><td>$150 – $1,000</td><td>Doors, hardware, caulk, drywall, fixtures</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Appliance clean or swap</td><td>$100 – $800</td><td>Deep clean vs replacement</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>What tier of turn does your unit need?</h2>
  <p>
    Not every unit needs everything above. In practice, turnovers fall into
    three tiers, which is exactly how we package them.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Light refresh (roughly $1,200 – $1,800):</strong> deep clean, paint touch-up, minor repairs, new filter. For a unit that was well kept and just needs to show well.</li>
    <li><strong>Standard turn (roughly $2,500 – $3,500):</strong> full repaint, carpet cleaned or replaced, all punch-list repairs, appliance deep clean, move-in inspection. The most common turn.</li>
    <li><strong>Full make-ready ($4,000+):</strong> new LVP flooring, cabinet painting, updated fixtures, the works. For a unit going after a rent bump.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    See how we structure these as flat-rate packages on our
    <a href="/turnovers/">unit turnover page</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>The cost every landlord forgets: vacancy</h2>
  <div class="takeaway">
    <p>
      The most expensive line item in a turnover is not on any invoice. It is
      the rent you lose while the unit sits empty. On an $1,800/month rental,
      every vacant day costs about $60. A turn that drags three weeks instead
      of one costs you roughly $840 in lost rent alone, on top of the work.
    </p>
  </div>
  <p>
    This is why a coordinated turnover beats hiring trades one at a time.
    Booking a cleaner, then a painter, then a handyman separately adds days of
    dead time between each visit while everyone plays schedule tag. One crew
    that covers every trade compresses the whole turn into 4 to 7 days, and
    the saved rent usually pays for the coordination. Deferred maintenance
    works the same way: skipping the small stuff now becomes a bigger turn
    later.
  </p>

  <h2>How to keep turnover costs down</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Paint in the same color, always.</strong> One standard wall color across your portfolio means touch-ups instead of full repaints for years.</li>
    <li><strong>Switch carpet to LVP once.</strong> It costs more up front but survives the next several tenants, so you stop paying for carpet every turn.</li>
    <li><strong>Do a mid-lease check.</strong> Catching a small leak or a failing seal before move-out keeps it off the turnover punch list.</li>
    <li><strong>Bundle the work.</strong> One crew, one flat quote, one schedule beats four separate service calls every time.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The bottom line</h2>
  <p>
    Budget <strong>$2,500 to $3,500</strong> for a standard Middle Tennessee
    turn, less for a refresh, more if you are upgrading flooring. Then add the
    vacancy math, because speed is money on a turnover. Upkeep runs
    <a href="/turnovers/">packaged make-readies</a> across Nashville,
    Brentwood, Franklin, and the rest of Middle Tennessee, with the number
    quoted flat before the unit is even vacant. One
    <a href="/work-order/">service request</a> gets you a turnover quote.
  </p>
</ArticleLayout>
